1
00:00:06,700 --> 00:00:08,908
Forty-five lines of Python. One PDF.

2
00:00:08,908 --> 00:00:13,570
And a local AI that answers questions about
it, with page citations, running

3
00:00:13,570 --> 00:00:15,349
entirely on your own machine.

4
00:00:15,899 --> 00:00:19,779
By the end of this video, you'll have built
this yourself, from an empty folder.

5
00:00:19,779 --> 00:00:23,853
And I'll show you the one mistake it made,
because that mistake teaches us more than

6
00:00:23,853 --> 00:00:24,920
all the right answers.

7
00:00:26,720 --> 00:00:28,488
Welcome back to CyberScripters.

8
00:00:28,488 --> 00:00:31,623
This is episode two of Build a
Secure RAG From Scratch:

9
00:00:31,623 --> 00:00:33,733
your first RAG, in under fifty lines.

10
00:00:35,533 --> 00:00:38,396
Quick recap. A RAG system has
two halves. Indexing:

11
00:00:38,396 --> 00:00:41,877
load the document, split it into
chunks, and embed each chunk.

12
00:00:41,877 --> 00:00:43,561
Then retrieval and generation:

13
00:00:43,561 --> 00:00:47,491
embed the question, find the closest
chunks, and hand them to the LLM.

14
00:00:48,041 --> 00:00:52,027
Today, each of those steps becomes
one small Python function:

15
00:00:52,027 --> 00:00:56,667
load_pdf, chunk, embed, retrieve, and
answer. That's the whole program.

16
00:00:58,467 --> 00:00:59,688
First, the setup.

17
00:00:59,688 --> 00:01:05,578
Clone the series repo, create a virtual
environment, and install the requirements:

18
00:01:05,578 --> 00:01:11,253
numpy, pypdf for reading PDFs, the Ollama
client, and optionally the OpenAI and

19
00:01:11,253 --> 00:01:12,690
Anthropic libraries.

20
00:01:13,240 --> 00:01:15,083
Then pull two models with Ollama.

21
00:01:15,083 --> 00:01:18,771
nomic-embed-text turns text into
vectors, and llama3.2 is a small,

22
00:01:18,771 --> 00:01:21,789
three-billion-parameter model
that writes the answers.

23
00:01:21,789 --> 00:01:24,582
Together, they're about two
point three gigabytes.

24
00:01:26,382 --> 00:01:29,944
Our data is the Acme Robotics
employee and customer handbook.

25
00:01:29,944 --> 00:01:34,439
Fifteen pages about leave, expenses, IT
security, support levels, refunds and

26
00:01:34,439 --> 00:01:35,081
warranties.

27
00:01:35,081 --> 00:01:39,868
Acme is fictional, so every fact is made up,
which means we can check every answer

28
00:01:39,868 --> 00:01:40,802
the model gives.

29
00:01:41,352 --> 00:01:42,647
Keep an eye on page twelve.

30
00:01:42,647 --> 00:01:45,381
That's the refund policy, and it's
going to matter later.

31
00:01:47,181 --> 00:01:49,861
Let's write the code. Step
one: load the PDF.

32
00:01:49,861 --> 00:01:54,149
pypdf gives us every page, and we keep the
page number next to the text.

33
00:01:54,149 --> 00:01:57,247
That page number is what lets us
cite sources later.

34
00:01:57,797 --> 00:02:01,646
Here's what page twelve looks like after
extraction. Notice the first two lines:

35
00:02:01,646 --> 00:02:04,149
the footer and the page number
ended up in the text.

36
00:02:04,149 --> 00:02:06,266
That's noise, and every chunk will carry it.

37
00:02:06,266 --> 00:02:08,431
We'll clean that up properly
in episode four.

38
00:02:10,231 --> 00:02:11,092
Step two: chunk.

39
00:02:11,092 --> 00:02:15,503
We slide a window of eight hundred characters
across each page, moving forward six

40
00:02:15,503 --> 00:02:19,969
hundred and fifty characters at a time, so
neighbouring chunks overlap by a hundred

41
00:02:19,969 --> 00:02:21,098
and fifty characters.

42
00:02:21,648 --> 00:02:25,010
The handbook becomes twenty-seven chunks.
Simple, but there's a catch.

43
00:02:25,010 --> 00:02:26,643
Look at how page twelve was split.

44
00:02:26,643 --> 00:02:30,390
The first chunk ends in the middle of a
sentence about Business plans, and the

45
00:02:30,390 --> 00:02:33,223
second chunk starts in the middle
of a word. Remember this.

46
00:02:35,023 --> 00:02:36,013
Step three: embed.

47
00:02:36,013 --> 00:02:40,464
One call to Ollama sends a list of texts to
nomic-embed-text, and we get back one

48
00:02:40,464 --> 00:02:41,344
vector per text.

49
00:02:41,344 --> 00:02:44,311
Each vector has seven hundred and
sixty-eight numbers.

50
00:02:44,861 --> 00:02:49,343
We embed all twenty-seven chunks once, at
startup, and keep the vectors in a numpy

51
00:02:49,343 --> 00:02:49,671
array.

52
00:02:49,671 --> 00:02:51,911
This is the real matrix for our handbook.

53
00:02:51,911 --> 00:02:54,753
For one PDF, that's all the
vector database we need.

54
00:02:56,553 --> 00:02:57,633
Step four: retrieve.

55
00:02:57,633 --> 00:03:01,792
We embed the question with the same model,
then compute the cosine similarity

56
00:03:01,792 --> 00:03:05,249
between the question and every chunk,
in a single line of numpy.

57
00:03:05,799 --> 00:03:09,326
argsort sorts the scores, we reverse
them, and keep the top four.

58
00:03:09,326 --> 00:03:12,039
These are the real scores for
our refund question.

59
00:03:12,039 --> 00:03:16,597
Both page twelve chunks come out on top, and we
return each chunk with its score, so

60
00:03:16,597 --> 00:03:18,930
we can see how confident the retrieval was.

61
00:03:20,730 --> 00:03:21,701
Step five: answer.

62
00:03:21,701 --> 00:03:25,912
We join the retrieved chunks into one context
block, each tagged with its page

63
00:03:25,912 --> 00:03:26,290
number.

64
00:03:26,290 --> 00:03:28,827
Then we wrap it in the prompt
from episode one:

65
00:03:28,827 --> 00:03:33,199
answer only from the context, say I don't know
if it isn't there, and cite pages.

66
00:03:33,749 --> 00:03:36,670
Notice that we call generate,
not Ollama directly.

67
00:03:36,670 --> 00:03:41,228
That's the small adapter from episode one, so
the same code works with a local

68
00:03:41,228 --> 00:03:42,689
model, OpenAI, or Claude.

69
00:03:44,489 --> 00:03:45,814
Finally, the main block.

70
00:03:45,814 --> 00:03:47,746
Load, chunk and embed the PDF once.

71
00:03:47,746 --> 00:03:52,274
Then answer the questions passed on the command
line, or keep asking interactively

72
00:03:52,274 --> 00:03:53,378
until you type exit.

73
00:03:53,378 --> 00:03:56,250
Print the answer, and the
sources with their scores.

74
00:03:56,800 --> 00:03:57,550
And that's it.

75
00:03:57,550 --> 00:03:59,853
Forty-five lines, including the blank ones.

76
00:04:01,653 --> 00:04:02,753
Let's run it. We ask:

77
00:04:02,753 --> 00:04:05,371
what's the refund policy for
enterprise customers?

78
00:04:05,371 --> 00:04:07,676
How fast this runs depends on your hardware.

79
00:04:07,676 --> 00:04:11,289
On my small two-core test machine, the
whole run took under a minute.

80
00:04:11,839 --> 00:04:13,006
And here's the answer.

81
00:04:13,006 --> 00:04:17,301
A full refund within thirty days of the
invoice date, prorated after that minus a

82
00:04:17,301 --> 00:04:21,649
ten percent fee, credit notes within fifteen
business days, and CFO approval above

83
00:04:21,649 --> 00:04:23,186
twenty-five thousand dollars.

84
00:04:23,186 --> 00:04:25,678
All correct, and page twelve
is the top source.

85
00:04:26,228 --> 00:04:27,529
But look at bullet four.

86
00:04:27,529 --> 00:04:31,865
Enterprise annual contracts refunded pro rata
within the first ninety days only.

87
00:04:31,865 --> 00:04:32,570
That's wrong.

88
00:04:32,570 --> 00:04:35,714
The ninety-day rule is for Business
plans, not Enterprise.

89
00:04:37,514 --> 00:04:38,555
Why did that happen?

90
00:04:38,555 --> 00:04:40,638
Remember the chunk that was cut in half.

91
00:04:40,638 --> 00:04:44,752
The Business plan sentence was split across
two chunks, and its first half sits

92
00:04:44,752 --> 00:04:46,471
right after the Enterprise rules.

93
00:04:46,471 --> 00:04:50,845
A small model saw annual plans refunded pro rata
within ninety days, and attached it

94
00:04:50,845 --> 00:04:51,782
to the wrong plan.

95
00:04:52,332 --> 00:04:54,654
This is the most important
lesson of the episode.

96
00:04:54,654 --> 00:04:57,307
RAG answers are only as good as
the chunks you retrieve.

97
00:04:57,307 --> 00:05:01,239
Bad chunk boundaries create confident, wrong
answers, even when retrieval finds the

98
00:05:01,239 --> 00:05:01,760
right page.

99
00:05:02,310 --> 00:05:06,756
We'll fix this with smarter, structure-aware
chunking in episode four, and we'll

100
00:05:06,756 --> 00:05:10,924
build tests that catch mistakes like this
automatically, in episode eleven.

101
00:05:12,724 --> 00:05:14,651
Let's try a few more questions.

102
00:05:14,651 --> 00:05:16,577
The warranty on the AX-200 arm:

103
00:05:16,577 --> 00:05:19,249
twenty-four months, page thirteen. Correct.

104
00:05:19,799 --> 00:05:20,495
Annual leave:

105
00:05:20,495 --> 00:05:24,564
twenty-four days, with up to five carried
over to the thirty-first of March.

106
00:05:24,564 --> 00:05:25,582
Correct, page five.

107
00:05:25,582 --> 00:05:28,901
And the server room is on the third
floor. Correct, page nine.

108
00:05:29,451 --> 00:05:32,924
Now a question the handbook can't
answer: what is the CEO's salary?

109
00:05:32,924 --> 00:05:34,634
And the model says: I don't know.

110
00:05:34,634 --> 00:05:38,054
That's exactly what we asked for in the
prompt. No made-up number.

111
00:05:39,854 --> 00:05:42,056
Want to use a cloud model instead?

112
00:05:42,056 --> 00:05:47,495
Copy the example env file, set LLM_PROVIDER to
anthropic or openai, and add your API

113
00:05:47,495 --> 00:05:47,754
key.

114
00:05:47,754 --> 00:05:52,416
Run the exact same command, and the
answers now come from Claude or GPT.

115
00:05:52,966 --> 00:05:55,229
The embeddings still run
locally with Ollama.

116
00:05:55,229 --> 00:05:59,404
Your documents only reach the cloud model as
part of the final prompt, meaning just

117
00:05:59,404 --> 00:06:00,460
the retrieved chunks.

118
00:06:00,460 --> 00:06:04,031
In episode twelve, we'll look at what
that means for confidential data.

119
00:06:05,831 --> 00:06:06,983
So, what's missing?

120
00:06:06,983 --> 00:06:10,806
First, we re-embed the whole PDF
every time the program starts.

121
00:06:10,806 --> 00:06:14,446
That's fine for fifteen pages, but
not for fifteen thousand.

122
00:06:14,446 --> 00:06:17,905
Next episode, we store vectors in
a real vector database.

123
00:06:18,455 --> 00:06:20,171
Second, our chunking is naive:

124
00:06:20,171 --> 00:06:22,860
fixed character windows, with
footers mixed in.

125
00:06:22,860 --> 00:06:24,748
And third, it only reads one PDF.

126
00:06:24,748 --> 00:06:28,753
Word documents, emails and Slack are
coming in episodes four to eight.

127
00:06:30,553 --> 00:06:33,813
The full code is on GitHub, on the
branch ep02. Next time:

128
00:06:33,813 --> 00:06:38,534
embeddings and vector databases, and why your
choice of embedding model matters more

129
00:06:38,534 --> 00:06:39,377
than you think.

130
00:06:39,927 --> 00:06:43,624
If you built this along with me, drop your
first question and answer in the

131
00:06:43,624 --> 00:06:44,068
comments.

132
00:06:44,068 --> 00:06:46,927
Subscribe to CyberScripters, and
see you in episode three.
